# Tiled grid evaluation of LC questions · `spec:nrz:lc-grid-tiles`

**Principle (LC evaluation is two graphs joined by bindings)** · `prin:nrz:lc-grid-contract`

An indexed LC evaluation, over a finite grid or an open stream, is a descriptor graph lowered to a computational graph through checked bindings under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-two-graphs`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`). Exact relation, domain, cover and evidence nodes are shared over admitted domains; varying inputs and outputs are indexed symbols bound to native float region versions. Kernel applications connect those versions, and decision nodes provide the sole numerical-to-descriptor return path (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-certified-feedback`). Every index's own enclosed answer is a selection of its output binding. Reuse follows graph identity and dependencies under (`spec:nrz:reuse-concept`). This document owns the common indexed-domain, certificate, binding, projection, decision and graph-cost rules, then specializes them to finite axes. The open-axis specialization (`spec:nrz:lc-streams`) owns the host-filled tile loan, publication order and bounded retirement contracts used by both shapes. The open requirements below delimit capabilities that existing LC covers do not establish.

## Measured standing · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-standing`

**Observation (Measurement conditions)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`

A throwaway cost measurement against main revision `ea2a94f`, with measured paths unchanged at source revision `bc1ad28388d2e9ebe268ff01f46ec9e19624b83b`, uses a release build, `f64`, one core of a Ryzen 9 5950X and one run per size. Work is the budget's exact count, distinct from elapsed time. The forward question varies L at every cell, fixes C to `1/4`, requests one time point and has cycle range `[0,0]`; the selected inputs make the L*C endpoint products rational squares. The supplied measurement evidence identifies no measurement date or repeated-run uncertainty, so neither is inferred.

**Observation (Rebuilding the forward question at every cell)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`

For grid populations of 10, 100, 1,000 and 10,000 cells, answering from scratch costs respectively 237, 256, 278 and 270 microseconds per cell, and whole-cell totals of 74,496, 79,071, 83,698 and 88,691 Work per cell. These totals include input construction, relation construction and native projection, as well as the cover. A separate measurement counting only `forward_cover` gives 69,661, 78,782, 83,737 and 88,640 Work per cell at populations 10, 10³, 10⁴ and 10⁵ respectively; its other slices are 2,233 Work for input construction, 5 for relation construction and about 2,600–2,750 for native projection. The two supplied population sequences are retained as measured; these are distinct slices, not paired totals to subtract or a relabelling of the original populations. Growing exact fractions account for the increasing Work; peak memory stays at 4.6 MB. `LcRelation::forward_cover` accounts for about 85 percent of elapsed time, rebuilding the exact cover at each cell; `LcRelation::new` and `LcForwardProjection::new` account for about four percent each. Extrapolating 270 microseconds to a billion cells gives about 75 core-hours; this is an extrapolation, not a measured billion-cell run. The supported forward contract is (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`).

**Observation (Whole-box and prepared-family controls)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-reuse-controls`

Answering one box over the whole L range costs 231 microseconds and 70,369 Work independently of the number of cells represented, but returns one wide enclosure. Reading members from one prepared zero-time cover through `LcTimeProjection::member` costs 7.3 to 8.9 microseconds and 2,213 to 2,800 Work per member. One `LcTimeProjection::tail` call encloses all members from index 15 onward in four microseconds. These controls demonstrate useful family reuse under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-zero-time-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`); a wide common enclosure supplies no cell-specific answer, and the measured member route still spends Work after preparation.

**Observation (Rational-square admission defect)** · `obs:nrz:lc-grid-square-refusal`

`LcRelation::forward_cover` returns `LcRelationError::Projection` when either extremal L*C product lacks a rational square root. Its private `inverse_root` in `crates/bindings/src/lc_relation/forward/rectangle.rs` constructs the exact positive root, then requires `as_rational()` before taking its reciprocal. A generic positive L by C grid is therefore refused almost everywhere by this adapter, despite the mathematical frequency being defined. The rational extremal-rate restriction is explicit in (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`). This domain restriction and the repeated exact work of (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`) are separate defects: speeding up the square-only path does not supply general positive LC evaluation.

## Shared indexed evaluation and finite tiles · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-tile-contract`

**Definition (Indexed domains and certified restrictions)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`

An indexed evaluation has an exact axis-domain node, indexed input and output symbols, and edges assigning supplied and queried roles in the original LC relation. A finite axis or finite axis product supplies a grid; an open ordered axis supplies a stream under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-axis`). An execution tile is a subdomain node with a checked inclusion edge to its parent axis and a physical support-box node enclosing every selected input support, with original joint constraints retained. Index-domain inclusion and physical-support containment are separate relations. A certified domain fixes the question schema, roles, axes, meanings, source-map family and physical support; several execution tiles may restrict that same authority. Its certificate applies throughout the support, including admissible values between samples. A parameterized restriction can share inclusion evidence; a new domain or unsupported restriction needs its own preparation. Execution partitions the original relation without replacing it or inferring existence from samples.

**Definition (Grid axis and indexed inputs)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-inputs`

A grid specializes (`def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`) to an exact finite axis descriptor or a finite Cartesian product of ordered axis descriptors under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`). Its domain indexes instances of the original LC relation (`def:nrz:lc-relation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`). Each varying input is an indexed symbol over that domain; its active tile restriction is bound to one native region, with companion symbols bound to their regions. The global axis domain does not require simultaneous materialization of all its numerical selections. Axes may vary L, C, initial phase, query time, phase-time observations, amplitudes, energy or deterministic supports. Supplied and queried roles are edges to the original relation node, not extra inferred constraints. A series adds a sample-axis descriptor and a shared epoch node. Shared uncertainty sources remain shared nodes with their original joint-domain edges under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-source-symbols`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); an axis product does not prove independence. Statistical moments alone supply no bounded-support edge (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-certified-sets`).

**Definition (Tile subdomain and inclusion)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-tile`

A finite grid tile specializes the subdomain of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`) to a sub-box of the finite axis ranges. Domain-inclusion and boundary-ownership edges assign every grid index to one tile even when physical support boxes overlap. Different source or schema families require separately admitted domain nodes. Host-filled input regions, including partially filled restrictions, use the same loan and handback edges as stream segments under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-input-loan`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-handback`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-partial-tile`); a grid does not require the engine to synthesize input values.

**Rule (Shared exact certificate edges)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`

Certified-domain preparation establishes evidence edges from the admitted domain to the original relation, complete projection cover, cycle-domain, branch-boundary, physical-admission and pi nodes. Each fact has explicit proven, assumed or open standing: physical input assumptions stay assumptions, checked cover/cut facts carry their proofs, and unresolved obligations remain open. A finalized certified tile has all evidence required by its requested conclusion. Checked existence or emptiness and structural verdicts require their own edges; an enclosure alone supplies none of them. Parameterized output and companion symbols and their lowering relations are prepared at the same scope. The certificate quantifies over the varying input coordinates under (`def:nrz:reuse-lambda`), rather than extending a ground point proof by assertion. Its nodes are shared by all indices and compatible tile restrictions in that domain. Preparing another execution tile reuses this certificate through valid inclusion edges; it does not recertify the same physical box. For a fixed box, schema, proof precision and branch complexity, numerical-family certificate preparation is independent of index population; this does not include discovering an index-to-value map or proving data-dependent decision strata under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`). Open-axis arrivals within that box use native containment under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-admission`). Uniform branch facts and complete alternative-family nodes retain their distinct meanings under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`).

**Rule (Shared nodes and hoisted numerical values)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-shared-nodes`

The original relation, exact pi/full-turn definitions, root-bracket proofs and admitted cover/branch schemas are shared nodes across every tile whose domain and dependency edges permit reuse. A child's inclusion proof transports valid parent evidence; tighter cuts add new evidence without copying or rebuilding the parent graph. Exact nodes independent of the grid index lower to a shared numerical prelude under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-hoisted-constants`), evaluated once per compatible node-and-version key in (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). Shared symbols may have several checked region bindings; equal float values alone create no sharing relation. A changed guard or source version prevents numerical reuse even if the same symbolic node survives.

**Rule (Authenticated pi authority and numerical prelude)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-pi-reuse`

Setup and refinement use eligible graph-authenticated Machin authority under (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-graph-publication]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-bound-cache-key]`), (`[CAS-rule:cas:pi-bound-cache-accounting]`), retaining original-target, context, family and live-dependency checks. A miss may require full validation, and a warm exact citation still consumes Work; both belong to the attempt that performs them. Reusing the same `PiBoundCertificate` object through `LcRelation::forward_cover`, `PiIntervalCover::check` and `pi.check` still runs full finite-sum validation and is not authenticated reuse. The prepared fast path of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`) permits neither full pi validation nor a charged exact pi citation per numerical cell or arrival. Hoisting the numerical pi import and prelude evaluation is a separate obligation from sharing exact pi proof authority. Observe full validation, authenticated citations, proof replay, lookup probes/hits/misses/refusals and numerical import/prelude evaluation separately under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`). The cold and warm Work in (`[CAS-ex:cas:pi-bound-cache-costs]`) have their own receipt scopes and cannot be subtracted from the distinct measured forward slices.

**Rule (Indexed native bindings with checked overlap)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`

All continuously varying values, deterministic bounds and numerical errors are native float arrays bound to indexed tile symbols under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-binding`). Bindings connect symbols and region-version selections many-to-many; every overlap requires a checked reading relation under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-checked-overlap`), and every heap entry has binding coverage under (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-total`). Original source, kind, unit, axis and map dependencies are checked once as a parameterized family. Native containment guards check each selected complete support against the tile's certified box, including conversion error. Cycle indices remain exact descriptor-domain parameters outside the floating heap. On the prepared path of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), array entries cause no new exact rational literals, scalar descriptor nodes or exact admission edges. Live version and containment checks remain numerical work. The required family admission is open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-binding`).

**Rule (Output symbols and bindings precede execution)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`

Each queried quantity is an output symbol in the descriptor graph indexed over the evaluation axis and restricted to the active tile, with sample or exact cycle axes where its family requires them. Lowering derives its sort, kind, unit, domains and companion-symbol relations and binds it to a reserved indexed result region before any kernel runs, following (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-result-symbols`), (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-companion-symbols`). Total-bound and numerical-error symbols bind to their own companion regions with checked target edges. Pending bindings become readable only on complete publication. Afterwards the result is resolved by output symbol and selected binding to the live region version under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-validation`); selecting an axis position uses that admitted binding without constructing another scalar descriptor. Family membership, branch association and feasibility remain evidence edges on the output/domain graph, not facts inferred from its numerical centers.

**Rule (Heap pass as a lowered subgraph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-heap-pass`

One tile pass executes the lowered computation subgraph of (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-lowered-subgraph`): a retained kernel schedule whose application edges map indexed input-region versions and shared prelude versions to indexed output-region and companion versions. Its bounded numerical stages evaluate all selected entries without symbolic evaluation per cell. Each output selection has its own freshly computed enclosure or family of enclosures from that selection's actual heap inputs, with total deterministic coverage and separately tracked numerical error. Import, arithmetic and restoration error occur once under (`thm:nrz:descriptor-binding-kernel-enclosure`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-error-composition`). No remembered value, interpolation or copied wide tile hull supplies the answer. The shared parameterized lowering and authenticated input containment justify the numerical output binding without adding exact nodes or edges for each cell, subject to (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`); any newly selected semantic attachment has its separate decision cost.

**Rule (Premise of the zero-exact-work pass)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`

A cell or arrival has zero exact Work, exact node/edge additions, descriptor constructions and CAS calls only when its numerical law and every semantic conclusion used by the pass already have checked whole-family authority over the complete admitted support, index restrictions and dependency scope. Selection then only authenticates a prepared instance through admitted native source/map, containment, companion and current-version checks; it neither proves a new cut nor constructs a residual certificate. A prepared numerical cover can support fresh numerical answers without deciding a threshold that its support straddles. Naming possible branches in advance does not prove actual membership. This concept charges post-arrival decision selection, enclosure/cut replay, stratum construction and selected-attachment publication to decision work whenever their authority is not already prepared. Current per-arrival lattice enclosure, selection and publication APIs are not evidence of the prepared native path: their origin replay, exact endpoint import, containment and CAS selection remain charged. Such work follows (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`) and (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`), including their CAS boundary, and may be proportional to the population even in a finite batch. No guarded native classifier is assumed to replace that boundary. A future guarded indexed-family judgement would require a separately checked theorem and native premise interface before broadening this guarantee; it is outside this acceptance claim. Exact restriction, partial-prefix, gap and outcome-domain preparation is attributed when performed, including after handback, and cannot be hidden by retrospectively calling it setup. Native evaluation and authentication costs remain visible even on the admitted fast path.

**Rule (Node identity and version-dependent authority)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`

Reuse is identified by descriptor node identity plus the bound input-region versions, extended by the complete domain, context, source/map, companion, representation, arithmetic and proof dependencies in (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-reuse-key`). The tile domain's inclusion, cover and boundary nodes belong to that dependency closure. Replacing an input or companion version invalidates dependent output bindings and decision strata through the edges of (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-invalidation`), (`rule:nrz:decision-invalidation`), even when center bytes agree. An immutable relation or certificate node survives when its premises still apply; it does not authenticate the new numerical version. Rebinding within that same family can reuse its exact authority after native validation. Appending a disjoint segment does not mutate earlier input versions; only outputs whose dependency closure includes a changed source or growing-domain aggregate need renewed attachment. Compatible information refinement and changed assumptions have distinct publication edges under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-refinement`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-invalidation`). Widened supports or changed premises need new admission edges. Output and companion bindings publish atomically under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-atomic-publication`); selected strata publish with their checked evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`). Failed publication leaves the affected output symbols unavailable, and archived theorem nodes retain historical authority under (`rule:nrz:decision-attachment-lifetime`).

**Rule (Splits add domains and evidence edges)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`

For either finite or open axes, when an active tile cannot certify its required cycle range, branch or threshold conclusion across its whole box, refine its enclosure evidence or add child subdomain nodes. Checked inclusion edges connect each child to the parent, a checked cover edge proves that their union covers the parent, and boundary-ownership edges connect them to shared cycle-face, branch-cut or threshold nodes. Children reuse the parent's relation, admission and valid proof nodes through these edges; they never rebuild that authority. A tighter bracket or newly settled cut adds only its missing nodes and proof edges. Enclosure tightening for the same exact target and input family follows (`rule:nrz:decision-refinement`); the containment and boundary proof standard remains (`spec:nrz:lattice-decisions`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-boundary-selection`). Child-domain subdivision additionally requires the new checked inclusion, parent-cover and boundary-ownership obligations of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`); the existing refinement rule alone supplies no subdivision theorem or scheduler. Settled strata retain their complete evidence; unresolved subdomains receive the extra exact work. Conservative enclosures can cause proof-induced splits even where the true answer is constant; measure that graph growth separately. Changing numerical values within an already certified branch does not add exact structure per cell.

**Rule (Termination and undecided strata)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`

A declared refinement-policy node bounds cumulative exact Work, numerical work, bytes, depth and attempts, and identifies allowed formats and deterministic error goals. Each split reduces an index subdomain or tightens an admitted physical subdomain and consumes the finite allowance. An uncertain support at a singleton index can still straddle a boundary; physical child domains retain inclusion and cover edges to that same support, not new independent sources. Increasing precision cannot remove physical uncertainty. Refinement stops at complete evidence or the applicable limit. An undecided stratum retains open obligation edges, its last valid enclosure as a bound region version, and a typed stop node for `BoundaryIntersection`, `UnsupportedCertificate`, `RefinementExhausted` or `DecisionBudgetExhausted` under (`def:nrz:decision-outcomes`). If no finite enclosure is justified, its complete candidate-domain node remains explicit. Such a stratum asserts no complement membership or guessed branch (`rule:nrz:decision-undecided-continuation`). Invalid meanings, stale dependencies, false evidence and representation failures have typed refusal nodes and unavailable output bindings, separately from mathematical undecided or impossible outcomes.

**Definition (Cost as graph growth and numerical evaluation)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`

Let N be the evaluated index population, T the finalized execution-tile count, D the admitted certificate-domain count, R the additional certificate/refinement attempts, S the decision-selection attempts and B the maximum resident index population. For a stream, N denotes a finite observed prefix, never an infinite materialization. Exact Work charges construction, checking, comparison and reuse during D certificate preparations, any T restriction setups, R refinements and S selections, including failed attempts and work that adds no retained node. Bit size, branch-domain complexity, proof precision and the map dimensions of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`) remain explicit. Charge domain/inclusion preparation at its actual time: prefill preparation, post-handback partial-prefix or gap restriction, and post-arrival outcome selection are separate observations. Parameterized sharing must be measured rather than assumed. Only a pass satisfying (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`) adds zero exact nodes/edges, consumes zero exact Work, invokes no CAS and constructs no descriptors per cell. Its graph growth consists of native region versions and kernel-application edges for the bounded indexed schedule, not a kernel graph cloned at each cell. Arithmetic, input scanning, native containment and version checks remain per-cell time. For fixed samples and output/branch arity, native full-output evaluation is linear in evaluated cells and heap storage is proportional to B plus bounded scratch and shared prelude regions; total cost also includes the measured setup, map, selection and refinement sums. Series length and materialized cycle extent are separate dimensions. Arbitrary data-dependent strata may require O(N) evidence even when each physical support is far from a threshold. That case is reported separately from certified-map region workloads and from prepared uniform-semantic controls; a per-cell exact graph fails the latter controls. Stream latency specializes this accounting under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-cost`), (`def:nrz:lc-stream-latency`); cumulative construction and resident graph size are distinct measurements.

**Rule (Bounded live subgraphs and retained evidence)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-streaming`

Finite-grid streaming is the bounded segment schedule of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-bounded-resources`), with the same host loans, concurrent tiles, atomic bindings and evidence retirement under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-inflight`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`). The finite parent axis supplies the terminal domain; publication order follows its declared axis order. Complete materialized outputs and exported evidence have explicit storage costs outside the bounded resident graph. Region decisions in the declared certified-map class of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`) evaluate covering-box bindings directly under its cover-complexity assumptions; arbitrary host-filled inputs retain their separately charged scans and decision evidence. This rule delegates the lifetime mechanism to the stream concept; (`req:nrz:lc-grid-bounded-execution`) is its finite-axis application, not another streaming architecture.

**Rule (Tile size changes cost within one question graph)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`

The extent of a tile domain is bounded by validity of its evidence edges, heap and scratch capacity, output-family extent and enclosure-error growth. Fixed extents, adaptive subdivision or adaptation under a capacity cap use the same domain and cut proofs. Smaller domain nodes can reduce overestimation while adding exact setup structure; larger domains amortize that structure only while their premises and requested error goal hold. The choice changes cost, never the original question graph, mathematical answer, branch coverage or boundary convention. Output enclosures may differ within the same tracked-error contract, and exhausted allowances can leave an undecided stratum instead of a settled one, never incompatible certified facts (`thm:nrz:decision-reproducibility`). Tile selection cannot relax input uncertainty or tolerance. When inputs are filled by the host, size also trades fill latency against amortized setup under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-tile-size`); the same cost applies to a host-filled grid. The exposed finite-axis policy remains (`open:nrz:lc-grid-size-policy`).

## Exact frequency without rational roots · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-frequency`

**Rule (Positive-root expression and its numerical binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-exact-frequency`

For L>0 and C>0 the descriptor graph retains the exact operation `omega0=(1 rad)/sqrt(L*C)`, with operand edges to the indexed L/C symbols and positivity, root and reciprocal-domain evidence under (`def:nrz:lc-relation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`). Its coherent-rate node is `a=omega0/(1 rad)`. Rational inputs can give an exact algebraic root; varying inputs use the same expression over the admitted parameter domain. Rational normalization is optional. Before execution, the frequency and error-companion symbols bind to indexed result regions; the heap evaluates product, positive root and reciprocal with outward error propagation. A numerical root never replaces the exact operation node, and a rational bracket is an enclosure-evidence node for that expression, not a surrogate expression. Rational squares remain simplifications, never admission preconditions.

**Rule (Certified rational brackets settle cover cuts)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-root-brackets`

For an independent positive box with rational coherent product bounds `0<p_min<=L*C<=p_max`, exact arithmetic can check positive rationals r and s satisfying `r*r<=p_min` and `p_max<=s*s`. Thus `sqrt(L*C)` lies in `[r,s]` and a in `[1/s,1/r]`; tighter endpoint brackets can be certified by the same squared inequalities. For correlated inputs these product bounds are outer coverage under the retained joint premises. Combine such rational bounds with independently checked pi bounds to enclose the original lifted phase divided by `2*pi rad`. Exact rational comparisons then certify endpoint cycle indices and completeness of the intervening range using the original floor convention (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-cycle-branches`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-affine-enclosure`), (`rule:nrz:lattice-floor-selection`). Signed time and correlated phase inputs need their own whole-box extrema under the same original constraints. Bracket overlap with a cut triggers refinement, a split or undecided; equality at an irrational face needs an exact identity or other checked equality proof, not a tolerance. Bracketing permits conservative complete covers without falsely claiming that every enclosed endpoint or branch is attained.

**Rule (Frequency proof and execution edges retain their owners)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-frequency-owners`

CAS checks positive-root, squared-bracket, pi, complete cycle-domain and cut evidence at tile scope; these are exact nodes and proof edges shared by compatible domains. Bindings checks original physical correspondence, quantity kinds, source maps and the relation between indexed symbols and lowered numerical laws. Scalar supplies product, root, reciprocal and pi enclosure laws; matrix validates native input/companion versions and coverage and executes their kernel edges. Feasibility, emptiness, overlap-tail and horizon facts need their own evidence edges from the original domain, not merely a nonempty bracket (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-horizon`). Generic irrational-root cover edges and their parameterized numerical lowering remain open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-irrational-cover`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`).

## LC questions and decisions · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`

**Rule (Time to phase series binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-forward-series`

For supplied L, C, initial phase and a time-series symbol, the tile's evidence edges certify the positive or quiescent domain, complete lifted-phase/cycle cover, timing correlations and applicable forward-horizon derivation. The output is a phase symbol indexed over the product of the tile axis and a second, sample axis, with branch-family structure where wrapping requires multiple arcs. Lowering binds that symbol and its deterministic-bound and numerical-error companions to indexed result regions before kernels run. The lowered subgraph shares its frequency evaluation across the sample axis where dependencies permit, then computes each selected phase enclosure from its own inputs with exact wrap-boundary ownership. A requested horizon is a separate output symbol with finite, infinite or open-domain evidence. The sample-axis and shared-clock nodes remain attached under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-time-queries`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-horizon`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); the independent fixture is (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-forward-input-boxes`). Changes of cycle or whole-circle regime refine the domain through (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`).

**Rule (Inductance output and alias-domain binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-inductance-query`

For a query in L, the tile certifies projection of the original observation and supplied-constraint graph, complete rate/product alias domains, positive divisors and remaining scale directions. The output L symbol is indexed over the tile axis and, when necessary, the retained alias-family domain, with total-bound and numerical-error symbols bound before execution to corresponding result regions. Shared product P and rate nodes retain their relation to the original observations. Supplied positive C permits `L=P/C`; positive amplitude ratio R permits `L=R*sqrt(P)`; energy and positive current amplitude permit `L=2*E/Ihat^2`. Checked lowering connects those operations to a heap subgraph using each input selection, preserving every other supplied constraint and error companion. The precise meanings and fixtures are (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-any-direction`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-known-element`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-amplitude-energy`). Energy alone leaves a free-scale node and its evidence under (`arg:nrz:lc-relation-energy-identifiability`), rather than an invented bounded L binding.

**Rule (Capacitance output and common-product binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-capacitance-query`

For a query in C, the tile certifies the original joint observation/alias graph and elimination domains. The output C symbol is indexed over the tile and retained alias axes, bound at lowering with its total-bound and numerical-error companions. Supplied positive L permits `C=P/L`; positive amplitude ratio R permits `C=sqrt(P)/R`; energy and positive voltage amplitude permit `C=2*E/Vhat^2`. Their kernel edges compute the indexed C enclosure regions from actual input versions. Simultaneous L and C outputs share the same product/rate and source nodes; two marginal bindings introduce no independence edge (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-known-element`), (`ex:nrz:lc-relation-amplitude-energy`). The restricted capacitor-interval route of (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-capacitance-implementation`) is evidence for its own graph, not a generic family lowering or a universal determinacy theorem.

**Rule (Phase to indexed time-family binding)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-inverse-time`

For a target-phase symbol and epoch node, the tile certifies the original joint phase/rate relation, complete cycle-index domain, ordering or ambiguous-numbering facts and any justified overlap tail. The output is an indexed time-family node over the tile axis and exact cycle domain. A member is a projection node of that family, and a tail is a subfamily node with its covering or existence evidence and a start-time output symbol. Their schemas and requested numerical selections bind before execution to result and error-companion regions; the heap pass does not construct one exact member node per cell. On an admitted branch, kernel edges enclose `t_n=(theta_target-theta0+2*pi*n rad)/omega0`. The independent rectangle specializes to `I_n=[2*pi*n/a_max,2*pi*n/a_min]` only for `n>=0`, `a_min>0` and exact starting and target phase zero under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-time-queries`); correlated inferred phase/rate retains its shared constraint nodes. The existing nonnegative member adapter is not extended to signed inverse-time evaluation by this specialization. Tail status and exact-frequency exceptions follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-overlap-tail`), (`conv:nrz:lc-relation-tail-status`). Finite numerical selections cannot truncate an unbounded family domain. `LcTimeProjection` supplies existing member/tail semantics; indexed numerical lowering without residual per-member exact work is open under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`).

**Rule (Projection verdicts are scoped evidence facts)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-projection-verdicts`

Structural determinacy and numerical certainty are separate facts attached to projection-domain or stratum nodes under (`def:nrz:lc-relation-determinacy`), (`def:nrz:lc-relation-certainty`). A tile cover has no per-index feasibility consequence without a parameterized witness or existence edge scoped to that input family. Alias-family nodes retain their original-constraint and verdict dependencies. Positive-amplitude and quiescent domain nodes obey (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-kinds`): at rest the frequency binding can remain defined while physical oscillation phase has a domain-outcome node. Certified empty, underdetermined, defined-domain, undecided and refused strata have distinct evidence and availability consequences. Tightened supports and added observations preserve information by inclusion and proof edges under (`req:nrz:lc-relation-refinement-monotonicity`), including open obligations after exhausted refinement.

**Definition (Decision predicate and boundary nodes)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`

A decision node declares its exact predicate, threshold symbol, units, ordered quantifier structure, index/window domain and retained joint physical-family domain before evaluation. The quantifier structure identifies each bound index or physical variable, its scope and dependencies, including conditional fibres; it distinguishes, for example, `forall i exists s P(i,s)` from `exists s forall i P(i,s)` for one shared source s. Unsupported structures are refused, never reordered or replaced by independent marginal domains. Its partition node connects to shared threshold, cycle-face and branch-cut nodes with strict/inclusive ownership under (`def:nrz:decision-partition`), (`rule:nrz:decision-cut-predicates`). It consumes authenticated output-enclosure bindings and the exact partition; a kernel comparison alone cannot add a semantic fact. At a declared universal quantifier, yes requires complete coverage and no a feasible counterexample; at an existential quantifier, yes requires a feasible witness and no complete exclusion, with inner quantified obligations discharged in their declared scope. A singleton is a special domain, not a different mechanism. Empty physical domains have explicit outcome nodes rather than vacuous physical assertions. Every conclusion retains separate enclosure and cut or witness evidence under (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-feasibility-witness`).

**Rule (Aggregation preserves shared-source quantifier order)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-quantifier-order`

For the same retained joint family, per-index universal physical coverage can establish `forall i forall s P(i,s)`, and a feasible counterexample refutes that assertion. Per-index feasible witnesses establish `forall i exists s P(i,s)` only with their index-dependent witness scopes; they do not establish a common realization. A claim `exists s forall i P(i,s)` requires one joint witness s satisfying every indexed obligation and all original source constraints, or a checked equivalent joint existence proof. With shared `s in [-1,1]`, the predicates `P(0,s): s>0` and `P(1,s): s<0` each have a witness, yet no common s satisfies both. Decision covers, continuations and aggregate finality retain this ordered structure; any valid quantifier exchange needs its own proof. Stream persistence under source narrowing additionally follows (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-finality`).

**Rule (Decision strata over tile indices)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-cell-decisions`

A decision node on an active tile, finite grid or published stream segment partitions its index domain into stratum nodes with checked cover and disjointness edges. Certified-yes and certified-no strata carry descriptor-layer facts with enclosure/cut or witness edges; an undecided stratum carries open obligations, a bound last-enclosure version and a typed stop under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`). These are graph strata, not scalar truth values or a mathematical third region complement (`rule:nrz:decision-undecided-continuation`). A uniform tile conclusion yields one shared fact only when proved over that whole stratum and its declared quantifiers. Nonuniform conclusions need charged child-domain or membership evidence. Selection of an index from an already proved stratum resolves its fact and indexed output binding without adding an exact fact for that cell, under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`); discovering the actual stratum from newly arrived values is separate charged decision work and may require per-index CAS replay. Refusal subdomains attach typed failure nodes and unavailable bindings and remain covered. Later continuations depend on the selected stratum through (`rule:nrz:decision-stratification`), (`rule:nrz:decision-stratum-sharing`); they cannot justify the enclosure that selected them. The graph-derived obligations of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`) refine the older table interface; this parameterized decision capability remains (`req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`).

**Rule (Original outcome obligations as graph-derived selections)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`

For admitted indexed families, this contract refines (`def:nrz:decision-outcome-table`) and (`rule:nrz:decision-batch-strata`): their stable original order, original point identity, parent stratum, decision identity, exact case/parameter association, actual input and companion versions, evidence, attempt/policy history, selected option or undecided/refusal reason, downstream activation and output-availability obligations all survive. The original key and ordinal are a view of the admitted axis-to-original association; the graph is not keyed by a second cell-result collection. Given an index and decision node, checked axis/source maps resolve that association, parameterized bindings resolve the actual version selections, and membership/coverage edges resolve outcome, original branch, evidence and downstream instance. Shared policy/attempt nodes and any exceptional domain edges preserve failed dependencies and inactive alternatives. When family authority and membership are already proved, this traversal constructs no new exact point instance or residual certificate. New selection evidence remains charged under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`). The admitted family replaces duplicated per-point exact authority and mandatory stored outcome rows with these total graph paths; it does not remove traceability or proof obligations, and older routes lacking this family contract retain their existing requirements. Any materialized outcome table is an optional charged view derived from the graph, including linear enumeration, bytes and version validation, never a second source of truth. Shared templates under (`rule:nrz:decision-stratum-sharing`) alone do not establish this replacement.

**Rule (Region decision graph and covering evidence)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-region-decisions`

A region decision is a decision node over a declared index/window or continuous physical domain with the ordered quantifiers and retained joint family of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-quantifier-order`). Open-axis finality additionally obeys (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-finality`). Bound covering-box enclosures feed a checked domain partition with branch/verdict evidence. At the relevant quantified scope, complete yes coverage proves universal yes and a feasible counterexample proves its no; a feasible witness proves existential yes and complete exclusion proves its no. Inner obligations and shared-source witness scopes cannot be exchanged while aggregating. Open strata retain unresolved boxes and obligations without forcing an aggregate verdict. Settled interiors avoid index enumeration only in the certified-map workload class of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`), where the regional map and cover evidence justify that omission. Arbitrary input arrangements can require scans or singleton subdivision far from any physical boundary. A finite grid partition has no continuous-region cover edge merely because all samples decide (`rule:nrz:decision-membership-locality`), (`rule:nrz:decision-batch-strata`). Requested point outputs bind over the strata's index ranges to result and companion regions, with explicit linear materialization cost. Existing decision/lattice containment rules supply the enclosure/cut proof standard; child-domain covers and the region scheduler remain open under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`).

**Definition (Certified-map class for boundary-sensitive cost)** · `def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`

A boundary-sensitive claim declares a workload class with an authenticated index-to-value map `F(i,s)` for the complete input supports and their joint source variables s, plus checked regional image enclosures and a cover-complexity bound for the selected index geometry and resolution. One admissible class is a fixed-dimensional, finitely described piecewise map whose certified regional image bounds and threshold geometry prove a cover bound `K0+K_boundary(N,epsilon)`: K0 bounds settled pieces independently of interior population, and K_boundary bounds unresolved pieces at declared resolution epsilon. That bound is a workload premise requiring evidence, not a consequence of ordered axes, monotonicity by itself or a common support box. Declare map description size, number of pieces, source/dependency complexity, index and coefficient bit sizes, discovery/admission/validation Work and time, regional image and membership-query cost, cover size/depth and retained/exported bytes. Include scanning needed to establish that host values follow the map, even if it occurs before evaluation. Uniform physical support means one admissible physical domain; uniform decision outcome means the same proved quantified conclusion; uniform index geometry means a compact cover in the admitted domain representation. None implies the others. Noncontiguous strata require a justified membership map with charged discovery and authentication. Without such a certified map and cover bound, arbitrary host-filled arrays and streams form a separate workload class with unavoidable input/decision scans and potentially O(N) stratum evidence, even if a batched native classifier is efficient.

**Counterexample (Alternating settled interiors)** · `cex:nrz:lc-grid-alternating-interiors`

Take a host-filled one-dimensional grid with `C=1`, `t=1`, `theta0=0`, positive energy and L alternating between `1` and `1/9`. The physical phase alternates between one and three radians, so every singleton support is away from a threshold of two radians, but every interval containing both values has a straddling physical hull. Sub-box refinement may need singleton index tiles despite no physical support touching that threshold. A certified parity map could describe noncontiguous strata compactly, but discovering or validating it against arbitrary host entries is charged map work under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`). This is distinct from the case in which every physical support itself straddles the boundary, and forbids an unconditional boundary-sensitive claim.

## Acceptance measurement · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance`

**Requirement (Rerun the measured workload over tile graphs)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-benchmark`

Rerun (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`) through tile-domain admission and the lowered heap subgraph at populations 10, 100, 1,000 and successive powers of ten through 100,000,000. Cover every question kind in (`sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`), both decision forms, rational-square controls and generic positive non-square L*C inputs. Retain the release `f64`, single-core workload for comparability and identify actual hardware, toolchain, arithmetic revisions and policies. Use repeated independent timings with raw observations, warm/cold setup state and a declared interval-estimation method; the one-run baseline supplies no retrospective intervals. Include domains sharing one certificate, tile-capacity crossings, cycle/branch/threshold boundaries, exact equality, correlated supports, quiescent domains, aliases and exhausted-refinement strata. Hold sample-axis extent and numerical branch/output extent fixed for primary cell-scaling fits, with separate controls varying those dimensions. All measurement outputs belong to acceptance evidence; they are not the representation of LC results.

**Requirement (Setup, exponent and exact graph attribution)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`

For each question kind and fixed workload/policy, fit total time `t(N)=k+c*N^p` and report k, c, p, units, uncertainty intervals, confidence level, residuals and raw independent timings. Measure setup directly and steady per-cell heap time; c is a per-cell cost only when p is one. Report certificate-domain count separately from execution-tile count, extents, capacity, new/shared exact nodes and edges, refinements, selections, measured setup-time sums and per-tile Work distributions. Separate preparation and already-prepared-pass fits expose repeated exact construction even when its coefficient is small. For nonconstant tile, map, decision and refinement setup, use the observed setup sum in `t_total(N)=k+sum_j setup_j(N)+c*N^p` and fit the prepared evaluation term separately, never a fictitious constant intercept; fit or stratify distinct capacity regimes. Before observations, predeclare the practical equivalence band or one-sided superlinear tolerance, confidence coverage, maximum interval width and model-adequacy rule under (`open:nrz:lc-grid-scaling-policy`). Full-output linear equivalence passes only if the entire relevant confidence interval lies within that band, or satisfies the declared one-sided criterion, as well as its width and adequacy requirements. Merely overlapping one is insufficient. A failed equivalence test or inadequate fit is inconclusive for a linear-scaling claim, not a structural proof; no finite experiment excludes every exponent above one. Use independent runs as inference units for fits, latency tails and cost ratios, retaining censored waits/stops rather than treating correlated readings as independent repetitions.

**Requirement (Graph counters, memory and exhaustive domain coverage)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-counters`

Count exact nodes and edges added, CAS invocations, descriptor constructions and exact Work at preparation, restriction, decision and refinement attempts, including failures. Zero per-cell claims require (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`) and the coverage/observation contracts of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`). Count separately native region versions and kernel-application edges, arithmetic, containment and current-version checks. Report heap bytes per resident cell, scratch, resident descriptor nodes/bytes, live authority references, absolute peak and exported graph/output storage while N increases at fixed capacity. Checked disjoint stratum covers establish exhaustive once-only accounting of completed, undecided and refused domains; no independent outcome census supplies that proof. Measure their cardinalities by reason, including non-square input, unsupported certificate, boundary, exhausted allowance, numerical range, invalid domain/meaning and stale evidence. Rational-square and boundary predicates remain independent of outcome classification. Failed and open domains remain in timing denominators. Include complete authority, companion versions, scheduling storage and historical registry/schema/provenance owners so retirement cannot conceal retained growth.

**Requirement (Accounting coverage for every measured route)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`

The acceptance evidence includes a coverage manifest for every entry point and helper traversed by each measured route, identifying caller-visible, private or discarded budget ledgers, charged operations, unmetered operations and observer coverage. A zero returned Work delta or no retained graph growth alone proves absence of neither symbolic reconstruction nor authentication nor copying. Explicitly cover context construction in `crates/bindings/src/engine.rs`, fresh private checking budgets in `crates/bindings/src/checker.rs`, `Binding::validate` and its immutable-budget allowance checks, symbol import preparation in `crates/matrix/src/binding/symbols.rs`, scalar `Enclosure` arithmetic/reductions, heap import/replacement/retirement, layout/shape validation and descriptor construction where used. Lattice freezing, enclosure, selection, preparation and publication take caller budgets and charge work even when they expose no `usage()` result; absence of that accessor does not make them unmetered. Observe deep owned clones, destruction and allocation events, capacity and bytes separately from conservative Work tariffs. The manifest is evidence about these routes, not the LC result model. A repository-wide public manifest, general public accounting API and expanded allocator inventory remain the accounting owners' separate scope; their absence cannot excuse gaps on a claimed route.

**Requirement (Exclusive phase receipts and honest remainder)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`

Provide exclusive receipts, or equivalent auditable snapshots on each same ledger, for preparation, proof generation/replay, lookup/authentication, native validation/evaluation and publication, with explicit remainder and refusal accounting. Phase totals plus remainder equal the observed ledger delta without double counting nested calls; retain private/discarded ledger deltas separately and explain cross-ledger attribution. Preserve spent Work on refused attempts and leave all existing Work pins unchanged. Separate unmetered time/events from charged Work rather than inventing a tariff. Place snapshots at the actual prefill, handback, decision, refinement and replay boundaries of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`). The test-only `work_trace.rs` is an attribution precedent with dynamic storage/search, not an implemented fixed-storage public observer. Any observer used for bounded-residence claims has its own measured storage/time and cannot hide an unbounded trace.

**Requirement (Construction and reuse events)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`

Observe attempted, completed and retained descriptor, exact-node, evidence-edge and certificate constructions, including transient or rolled-back work. Separate full pi validations, graph-authenticated exact citations, portable and local proof replays, lookup probes/hits/misses/refusals and numerical pi import/prelude evaluations under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-pi-reuse`). Count meaningful deep copies, allocations, capacity growth, retirement and destruction with bytes/time where applicable; also expose legitimate native containment, source/map and version authentication. Net graph growth and cache-hit counts alone are insufficient. Zero prohibited events on an admitted fast path must agree with the phase receipts and coverage manifest, while permitted native work remains reported.

**Requirement (Setup, answer and replay claim scopes)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-claim-scopes`

Every setup, answer or replay receipt identifies its original relation/family, index and physical coverage, source/map/companion versions, authority revision and returned claim. A whole-box cover, a fresh point-specific numerical answer, a selected decision and a portable proof replay have separate scopes even when they cite one certificate. The acceptance scenario prepares one support certificate, separately obtains its whole-box cover and fresh point-specific answers from actual inputs, then demonstrates zero prohibited construction/replay events only on passes admitted by (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), while reporting native checks. Mutate an original source, map and unchanged-center companion, exercise an outside support and an equal-valued foreign source, and require refusal or correctly scoped revalidation; unchanged numerical values cannot authenticate changed dependencies. Report portable proof replay independently. Use the zero-time family as a positive control for cover reuse with its nonzero exact member Work visible, the spectral batch as a control for shared schema/prelude and live validation only, repeated current `forward_cover` as a negative control for pi reuse, and graph-authenticated Machin authority as its positive control. None of these controls alone proves general indexed decisions.

**Requirement (Joined decision-attempt evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-decision-attempts`

Decision workloads join observations of preparation, origin replay, containment, selection and selected publication for the same original question, dependency versions and attempt. Retain failed and undecided attempts with their stop/refusal reasons, spent resources and last valid enclosure; a successful selected attachment does not erase earlier costs. Existing lattice snapshots can supply this evidence where complete under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`). A general public lattice report belongs to its owner and is not a prerequisite to forward-only controls, but full decision acceptance requires this joined observation and the enclosure/cut and atomic-publication obligations of (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`).

**Requirement (Small reproducible accounting fixtures)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-fixtures`

Publish small reference fixtures at at least two populations with exact input generation, the full L sequence or a unique generator, pi certificate revision/content, source and arithmetic revisions, snapshot positions, claim scopes and per-cell Work vectors. The measured conditions of (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-measurement-conditions`) do not uniquely determine the throwaway baseline's exact inputs or certificate; preserve its figures without claiming exact Work reproduction until those data are supplied, and identify any replacement comparison fixture as such. Raw timings supplement these fixtures and do not replace them. This requirement is scoped acceptance evidence, not a demand for a general benchmarking framework.

**Requirement (Decision strata and boxes against boundary share)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`

Separate the certified-map class of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`) from arbitrary host-filled arrays and streams. For the former, declare and justify its regional image and cover-complexity assumptions, then measure map preparation/validation/query costs, covering-box evaluations, settled/unresolved strata, child depth and exact nodes/edges/Work against N and the cells intersecting a declared neighborhood of the true decision boundary. Define that neighborhood from physical support, requested resolution and certified error, not observed failures. Vary resolution at fixed geometry, interior volume at fixed boundary resolution and boundary complexity, including wholly settled outcomes and exact faces. Growth may be claimed to follow the certified cover bound only with all map costs exposed. Separate region evaluation from optional indexed output materialization and input scanning. Arbitrary arrays/streams retain their unavoidable input/decision scans, membership discovery and any O(N) stratum evidence; a batched native classifier is not evidence of omitted interior enumeration. Include (`cex:nrz:lc-grid-alternating-interiors`) separately from the worst case of all physical supports straddling the boundary. Report physical-support uniformity, decision-outcome uniformity and index-cover geometry separately; none is a proxy for another.

**Criterion (Structural acceptance is pass or fail)** · `crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`

Acceptance requires zero exact nodes/edges, CAS calls, descriptor constructions and exact Work per cell on the admitted prepared passes of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), substantiated by (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-claim-scopes`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-decision-attempts`) and (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-fixtures`); no working-heap growth with total grid size at fixed capacity; and an adequate full-output fit meeting the predeclared equivalence criterion of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`) at fixed numerical/output complexity. Region-only boundary-sensitive acceptance applies only to the declared certified-map class and its proved cover assumptions under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`). Uniform-semantic controls with prepared membership must show population-independent exact authority graphs and Work; renaming an exact per-cell graph as singleton tiles fails those controls. Arbitrary data-dependent decisions have separately reported scan, selection and stratum costs, and cannot claim the same guarantee merely from uniform physical support. Checked covers retain every index and honest outcome, and generic positive non-square inputs cannot be refused merely for irrationality. Independent oracles and mutation/boundary controls verify semantics alongside accounting. Region classification need not have a linear exponent; optional output materialization has its separately tested linear cost. These are future acceptance obligations, not measured results of this concept.

**Criterion (Uncertified ratio is a non-blocking target)** · `crit:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-target`

Measure plain uncertified `f64` evaluation of the same closed form, inputs, output-axis extent, branch convention, hardware and scheduling conditions. Report absolute per-cell time and the certified steady per-cell cost divided by that baseline, with intervals; distinguish exact graph setup, decision refinement and numerical output materialization. Prevent dead-code elimination and keep array traversal comparable. The owner chooses the multiple under (`open:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-policy`); this concept establishes none. The target is non-blocking and cannot weaken coverage, exclude unresolved strata from costs or replace (`crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`).

## Existing structures and open capabilities · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-engine`

**Rule (Existing covers become shared graph authority)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-covers`

`LcRelation`, `LcForwardCover`, `LcForwardProjection` and pair/scale projection owners provide existing relation, cover and branch authority under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-forward-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-pairs-implementation`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-scale-implementation`). The tile graph references that authority under its original premises rather than duplicating it per index. `LcTimeProjection` supplies complete member/tail semantics and existence strength under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-zero-time-implementation`). Existing owners do not yet supply arbitrary indexed live bindings, parameterized tile-domain evidence or zero exact graph growth per member; (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-reuse-controls`) measures the remaining Work. Family nodes with shared member/tail projection schemas, rather than exact construction at every numerical selection, are the open requirement.

**Rule (Existing execution contracts and graph boundaries)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-execution`

Indexed grid symbols, predeclared result/companion symbols, checked overlap, hoisted constants and the lowered computation subgraph carry over from (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-grid-symbol`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-result-symbols`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-checked-overlap`), (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-hoisted-constants`), (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-lowered-subgraph`). Plans and batches contribute dependency, source and bounded-execution contracts under (`def:nrz:computation-plan`), (`rule:nrz:plan-resource-ownership`), (`rule:nrz:batch-variation`); their grid realization here is the indexed graph binding. LC spectral batches supply existing shared-schema/prelude precedents (`rule:nrz:lc-resonance-plan-strata`), (`rule:nrz:lc-plan-bindings`), (`obs:nrz:lc-plan-spectral-executor`). Scalar laws, matrix version nodes, kernel edges and shared `PiConstant` supply numerical ingredients, while decision nodes retain exact enclosure/cut proofs and strata (`rule:nrz:decision-enclosure-and-cut`), (`rule:nrz:decision-stratum-sharing`). The spectral precedent retains a point table and has no decision continuation, while stratum sharing shares templates rather than point-specific residual certificates. The graph-native replacement requires (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`); none of these existing contracts alone implements arbitrary LC tile projection, family-selected authority or region refinement.

**Requirement (Parameterized tile authority graph)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-tile-authority`

Open capability, owned by CAS for mathematical authority and bindings for LC correspondence: admit tile-domain and physical-box nodes with inclusion, relation, complete-cover, cycle-domain, boundary and admission evidence edges, quantified over varying parameters. Share the original relation, pi, bracket and valid ancestor authority across compatible domains; add only missing refinement proofs. A point proof or schema-cache hit cannot supply a whole-family edge. All four projection directions require scoped feasibility or unresolved-domain evidence. This realizes (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-shared-nodes`) without choosing graph storage, a proof algorithm or a new implementation API.

**Requirement (Indexed input and output bindings)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-binding`

Open capability, owned by bindings and matrix: connect tile-axis input/output and companion symbols to native region-version selections once per admitted family, many-to-many with checked overlap, original source dependencies and total heap coverage. Bind every requested output before kernel execution under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`). Supply native containment and version authentication without adding exact literals, per-cell descriptors or admission edges. Equal-valued foreign source nodes, changed maps and widened companion regions must not inherit a tile's authority. This realizes (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`) within the existing binding contracts.

**Requirement (Irrational-root cover evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-irrational-cover`

Open capability, owned by CAS and bindings: retain exact positive-root frequency nodes on positive non-square product domains and attach complete cycle-range and branch evidence using original-target bracket or equality nodes under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-root-brackets`). Share validated brackets and eligible authenticated pi authority wherever domain-inclusion and original-target checks permit, with the observable reuse and separate numerical prelude obligations of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-pi-reuse`). Unsettled cuts keep open obligations and complete candidate-domain nodes. Positive nonsquare fixtures, exact-face equalities, zero-excluding reciprocal premises and corrupted bracket/pi evidence delimit acceptance. This replaces rational-root admission in the cover contract without inserting approximate constants into the descriptor graph.

**Requirement (Indexed projection subgraphs)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`

Open capability, owned by bindings for correspondence, scalar for primitive laws and matrix for execution: lower every query in (`sec:nrz:lc-grid-questions`) to an indexed kernel subgraph with all output and companion bindings declared first. Include frequency, sample-axis phase series, both element outputs, correlated alias/time-family projections, members, tails and horizons wherever certified. Numerical edges consume region versions and produce each output selection's enclosure without exact graph growth or CAS invocation per cell under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`); newly inferred semantic selections have separately charged evidence. Exact cycle-domain parameters keep admitted bounded numerical readings when arithmetic requires them; floats cannot decide their identity. Unsupported laws leave open obligations. Existing scalar and spectral kernels are ingredients, not evidence of complete family lowerings.

**Requirement (Decision strata and refinement edges)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`

Open capability, owned by CAS, bindings and the decision/plan owner: connect exact partitions and boundaries with authenticated enclosure bindings to decisions producing checked stratum covers. Attach whole-family enclosure/cut or existence proofs to yes/no strata, and open obligations, last-enclosure bindings and typed stops to undecided strata. Child subdivision additionally needs checked inclusion, parent-cover and boundary-ownership evidence under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-split`). Supply the total graph-derived outcome paths of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`), preserving original identities, versions, branches, policies and failed dependencies. Support declared continuous-region and grid-index quantifier structures without exchanging shared-source scopes under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-quantifier-order`). New data-dependent selection follows the existing CAS enclosure/cut and complete selected-publication boundary and is charged under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`), even when a batch amortizes it. Prepared numerical authority or native classification alone cannot mint semantic edges. Boundary-sensitive cost additionally requires the certified map and cover bound of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`); arbitrary per-index evidence is allowed only with its honest cost and narrower claim.

**Requirement (Bounded live graph and dependency retirement)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-bounded-execution`

Open capability, owned by matrix and the plan/batch owner: realize (`req:nrz:lc-stream-bounded-execution`) on finite axes, including host-filled grid tiles and resolvable evidence after buffer reuse. Apply (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-streaming`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`) with checked cover edges for completed, refused and undecided subdomains; account for the finite terminal domain and optional full output materialization. The shared bounded live graph, pending-loan, backpressure and retirement obligations are stated once in the stream requirement. No scheduler, cache container or wire representation is chosen here.

**Requirement (Auditable graph cost and semantic evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance-evidence`

Open capability, owned by the measurement and participating engine owners: supply workload families, independent numerical expectations, exact/native graph counters and uncertainty intervals under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-benchmark`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-counters`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`). Supply the route coverage, phase receipts, construction events, scoped answers/replays, joined decision attempts and reproducible fixtures of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-claim-scopes`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-decision-attempts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-fixtures`). Test graph sharing across tile-size and ordering variants, generic roots, exact boundary nodes, source-version invalidation and exhausted-refinement strata. Checked stratum covers must account for every original index and every possible branch; an unimplemented question has open requirements and cannot pass through absent successful outputs. This concept adds no code, tests, dependency, limit, default or literal Work pin.

**Rule (Acceptance scope is a declared capability claim)** · `rule:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance-scope`

A declared capability subset has evidence only for its named query forms, ordered quantifiers, workload classes, lifecycle operations and claim scopes; omitted obligations remain open. Full acceptance requires every listed obligation in this concept and its stream specialization, including graph-derived decision outcomes, charged data-dependent selection and the scoped accounting controls. Delivery sequencing and selection of implementation increments belong to a separate planning record, not to these architectural invariants. Measurement protocol, thresholds and supported subsets are fixed before their acceptance observations.

## Choices requiring the owner · `sec:nrz:lc-grid-owner-choices`

**Open Question (Ratio target multiple)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-policy`

Options are one common multiple of measured uncertified `f64` cost or separate multiples by question/output complexity, with the numerical values chosen after measurement. Recommended position: measure each kind first, then select per-kind multiples as non-blocking engineering targets. Different family and output-axis extents make an unmeasured universal number misleading. The owner supplies the multiples; structural graph acceptance remains (`crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`).

**Open Question (Tile-domain extent policy)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-size-policy`

Options are fixed user extents, automatic adaptive extents, or adaptive child-domain refinement under an explicit resident-memory cap with a deterministic fixed-extent override. Recommended position: the capped adaptive policy with the override, because proof boundaries, map cover geometry and error growth vary while bounded live graphs and reproducible comparisons need explicit controls. Declare whether a workload meets (`def:nrz:lc-grid-map-workload`); adaptation cannot make an arbitrary alternating interior boundary-sensitive or erase its map/stratum costs. Host-filled tiles also retain partial-suffix ownership and recovery capacity under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-suffix-obligation`). The owner chooses the exposed cap and geometry policy; no new default or capacity is established.

**Open Question (Refinement and publication of output bindings)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-refinement-policy`

Options are subdivision at one format only, a declared precision sequence followed by subdivision, or caller-selected bounded policies; output bindings can publish by completed stratum or after the whole requested domain settles. Recommended position: caller-visible finite policies with explicit error goals and publication of complete bindings over settled or explicitly undecided strata. Open obligations and refusal nodes remain attached to unresolved domains. This keeps physical uncertainty distinct from numerical ambiguity and preserves bounded execution. The owner chooses formats, ceilings and publication mode; policies cannot drop branches, relax deterministic coverage or move post-arrival restriction/decision work into prefill setup under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`).

**Open Question (Decision-domain scope and predicate interface)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-decision-policy`

Options are grid-index domains, continuous-region domains or both, with either a declared fixed quantifier order or an explicit supported ordered-quantifier interface. For data-dependent outcomes, options are charged selection under the current CAS boundary or a separately specified and checked guarded indexed-family judgement. Recommended position: expose both domains with mandatory index/joint-family scope and explicit supported quantifier order, retain yes/no/undecided and distinct domain/refusal outcomes, and charge new selection under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-fast-path`). This preserves shared-source meaning without claiming an unproved native decision theorem. Require graph-derived original-outcome paths under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-outcome-view`), with optional charged table views, because template sharing alone does not replace per-point obligations. Declare certified-map versus arbitrary-input cost scope separately. The owner chooses the supported interface/subset under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance-scope`); no choice exchanges quantifiers, drops traceability or extends a point proof to a family.

**Open Question (Acceptance protocol and capability scope)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-evidence-policy`

Options are one pinned reference environment or several reported environments, and evidence for a declared capability subset or the full capability contract. Recommended position: one pinned single-core release `f64` environment for cost comparison, both supported numerical formats for correctness, and explicit capability/workload/claim scopes under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-acceptance-scope`). This makes comparisons reproducible without attributing absent evidence to unsupported queries. Adopt all route-scoped accounting proposals in (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-coverage`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-phase-receipts`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-construction-events`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-claim-scopes`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-decision-attempts`) and (`req:nrz:lc-grid-accounting-fixtures`): auditable phase observation is sufficient without requiring a new public API, but missing coverage cannot support a zero-work claim. The owner fixes independent-run repetitions, confidence coverage, censoring and model diagnostics before observations, using (`open:nrz:lc-grid-scaling-policy`) for the operational equivalence threshold. Implementation sequencing belongs outside the concept.

**Open Question (Practical equivalence to linear scaling)** · `open:nrz:lc-grid-scaling-policy`

Options are a two-sided band around exponent one or a one-sided superlinear tolerance tied to the largest tested population. Recommended position: predeclare a two-sided band `[1-delta,1+delta]` with `delta=log(1+eta)/log(N_max/N_min)` for a chosen tolerable multiplicative deviation `eta>0` over a declared span `N_max>N_min>0`, a confidence coverage such as 95 percent and maximum interval width no greater than delta. This ties exponent tolerance to an interpretable scale effect rather than mere overlap with one. Require the whole confidence interval inside the band, positive fitted coefficient, measured setup sums and an adequate model in each declared capacity regime; recommend a predeclared residual lack-of-fit rule calibrated from independent replicate variation and refusal of systematic size trends. The owner fixes eta, coverage, width, repetitions, regime boundaries and the residual test with its threshold before observing results. A one-sided alternative must equally predeclare its upper bound and width/adequacy rules. No numeric tolerance or statistical default is established here; failed or inadequate evidence is inconclusive under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`).
